# tools/in/index_row_bound_witness.rish -- an index row points; it does not summarise.
#
# WHY THIS RUNG. REDS %204: `session-logs/README.md` folded correctly and still stood 5,421 bytes
# above the 24,576 it declares. The fold was never the problem. The rows were -- 36 of them
# averaging 783 bytes, the longest 2,223, with the meaning column carrying 655 of the 783. That is
# the session log's own abstract copied into the index, a second copy of a record the tree already
# keeps single-stranded: THE LOGS ARE THE RECORD, THE INDEX IS THE WAY IN.
#
# Keaton chose shorter rows on `20260824`. This rung is the number that makes the choice checkable.
#
# WHY IT READS THE OPEN SHELF, from `20260830` (REDS %381). This rung's own sentence has always been
# "this gates the one file a lap appends to," and `20260827.171500` moved that file. A log is now
# born on its day's shelf and its row prepended to `<room>/date/README-index-YYYYMMDD.md`, so the
# pin holds one row per DAY and every row a lap writes lands on the shelf. Pointed at the pin alone
# the scan measured **rows=0** and reported `verdict=ok` for four days, while the open shelf carried
# 87 rows for 64 logs -- six over the bound, eleven links resolving to no file, and 23 duplicate
# stamps, four of them a rebase re-applied over a corrected row. A guard reading an empty set is
# greener than a guard that is right, and only the count told the two apart.
#
# THE BOUND, derived rather than chosen. A row costs about 123 bytes before it says anything: 21 for
# the stamp cell, roughly 97 for the title-and-filename link, 5 for the pipes. The pin's own bound is
# 24,576 and its prose takes about 2,100, leaving roughly 22,400 for rows. At **192 bytes** -- a
# power of two beside the 256-file room bound and the 24,576-byte pin bound -- the pin holds about
# 116 rows and a meaning gets ~69 characters, which is a clause that points rather than a paragraph
# that restates.
#
# WHAT IS PROVEN, on metal. Four readings over the living pin AND the open shelf:
#   every row at or under 192 bytes; every row's link resolving against the page's own directory;
#   no two rows sharing a stamp, since a duplicate is one log wearing two rows; and the rows
#   descending, since a page titled newest-first keeps that promise in its own document order.
#   Forty behaviors on planted pins and planted shelves in a throwaway pen: the bound free at
#   exactly 192 and bitten at exactly 193, short rows free, one long row among three found and
#   named, the longest reported, a header row and a delimiter row and prose carrying pipes all read
#   past, a hyphen-form stamp read past because that shape is a filename rather than a row's first
#   cell, a CLOSED shelf carrying all three faults left free and uncounted, the OPEN shelf bitten by
#   each of the three readings and freed by each of their repairs, a `through-` gathering never
#   mistaken for the open day, a room with no shelf yet reading `none` rather than refusing, an
#   absent pin refused rather than read as zero rows and called clean, the bound itself readable
#   so a control can plant against it, a descending shelf free and counted at zero, a single rise
#   bitten and counted and its seam named from both sides, a tie left to the duplicate reading so
#   one fault is never counted twice, and a CLOSED shelf out of order left free and uncounted.
#
# THE FOURTH READING, and why it took a third firing to be built (REDS %440, then %445's lap). A
# rebase auto-merged two shelf rows landing four minutes apart -- cleanly, no marker, nothing to
# resolve -- and seated the OLDER above the newer. Every reading here stayed green through it,
# because a merge that reports no conflict has still made a decision. Measured on the 20260905
# shelf before the repair: 53 rows, TWO rises, three firings in one evening on one file. The
# closed-shelf freedom needed no new exemption -- a closed shelf never enters the read set, so the
# new gate inherited accrete-never-break from where it stands, which is what a part standing free
# buys.
#
# WHAT PASSES FREE, by named rule. A CLOSED shelf -- any `README-index-<day>` that is not the newest
# -- is immutable once its day closes and keeps every byte it wrote (accrete-never-break).
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX, and it is the honest half. The room bound is 256 flat files and a
# meaning-free row still costs ~123 bytes, so 256 rows need ~31,500 -- above the pin's 24,576
# whatever a row says. **The two seated bounds cannot both hold at the room's own ceiling**
# (REDS %205). This bound carries a median day comfortably: across 61 measured days the median is
# 61 logs and the mean 75, while 15 of those days ran past 116 and the peak reached 223. On such a
# day the pin exceeds again, and the remedy is a fold rather than a shorter sentence.
#
# Style: context/GAUGE_STYLE.md - Rule: .claude/rules/session-logs.md
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/in/index_row_bound_witness.rish

say "index-row-bound: Language EN -- an index is the way in, and the log is the record."
say "index-row-bound: Style Gauge -- the bound comes from the pin's own arithmetic rather than from taste."
say "index-row-bound: Lens TAME -- bound everything, and say where the number came from."

let scan = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/i/index_row_bound_scan.sh"]
assert scan.ok else "index-row-bound: a living index row stands above the 192 bytes an index row is given, points nowhere, or shares its stamp"
say scan.out
assert scan.out contains "rows_over=0" else "index-row-bound: every living index row must point rather than restate"
assert scan.out contains "rows_unresolved=0" else "index-row-bound: a row that points nowhere is a way in that is not one"
assert scan.out contains "rows_duplicate=0" else "index-row-bound: two rows carrying one stamp are one log wearing two rows"
assert scan.out contains "rows_misordered=0" else "index-row-bound: a page promising newest first must descend in its own document order"
assert scan.out contains "verdict=ok" else "index-row-bound: the scan must balance before it is believed"
say "field GREEN -- the pin holds one day of pointers and the open shelf holds rows that point"

let control = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/i/index_row_bound_control.sh"]
assert control.ok else "index-row-bound: the control must complete"
say control.out
assert control.out contains "at_bound_free=yes" else "index-row-bound: a row of exactly 192 bytes must pass free"
assert control.out contains "at_bound_measured=yes" else "index-row-bound: the scan must measure the planted length exactly"
assert control.out contains "over_bound_bitten=yes" else "index-row-bound: a row of 193 bytes must red, so no override exists"
assert control.out contains "over_bound_counted=yes" else "index-row-bound: the count must name how many rows stand over"
assert control.out contains "rows_counted=yes" else "index-row-bound: the row count must be the count of rows"
assert control.out contains "short_rows_free=yes" else "index-row-bound: short rows must pass free"
assert control.out contains "one_of_three_found=yes" else "index-row-bound: one long row among short ones must be found"
assert control.out contains "over_row_named=yes" else "index-row-bound: an over-bound row must be named, so it can be shortened"
assert control.out contains "longest_reported=yes" else "index-row-bound: the longest row must be reported at every pass"
assert control.out contains "non_rows_read_past=yes" else "index-row-bound: a header, a delimiter, and prose with pipes are not rows"
assert control.out contains "non_rows_free=yes" else "index-row-bound: reading past a non-row must leave the verdict green"
assert control.out contains "hyphen_stamp_read_past=yes" else "index-row-bound: a hyphen-form stamp is a filename, never a row's first cell"
assert control.out contains "closed_shelf_free=yes" else "index-row-bound: a closed shelf keeps every byte it wrote"
assert control.out contains "closed_shelf_uncounted=yes" else "index-row-bound: a closed shelf's rows must not be counted against the living bound"
assert control.out contains "open_shelf_named=yes" else "index-row-bound: the pass must name which shelf it read as open"
assert control.out contains "open_shelf_bound_bitten=yes" else "index-row-bound: the open shelf is the file a lap appends to, so the bound must reach it"
assert control.out contains "open_shelf_bound_counted=yes" else "index-row-bound: an over-bound shelf row must be counted"
assert control.out contains "unresolved_bitten=yes" else "index-row-bound: a row pointing at no file must red"
assert control.out contains "unresolved_counted=yes" else "index-row-bound: the count must name how many rows point nowhere"
assert control.out contains "unresolved_named=yes" else "index-row-bound: an unresolved row must name its target, so it can be repointed"
assert control.out contains "resolved_free=yes" else "index-row-bound: a row read from its own page's directory must resolve free"
assert control.out contains "duplicate_bitten=yes" else "index-row-bound: two rows carrying one stamp must red"
assert control.out contains "duplicate_counted=yes" else "index-row-bound: the count must name how many stamps stand twice"
assert control.out contains "distinct_stamps_free=yes" else "index-row-bound: two rows carrying different stamps must pass free"
assert control.out contains "through_not_open=yes" else "index-row-bound: a through- gathering names no day and is never the open shelf"
assert control.out contains "through_free=yes" else "index-row-bound: a through- gathering keeps every byte it wrote"
assert control.out contains "no_shelf_none=yes" else "index-row-bound: a room with no shelf yet must read none"
assert control.out contains "no_shelf_free=yes" else "index-row-bound: a room with no shelf yet must pass rather than refuse"
assert control.out contains "absent_pin_refused=yes" else "index-row-bound: an absent pin must refuse rather than read zero rows and call it clean"
assert control.out contains "bound_readable=yes" else "index-row-bound: the bound must be readable, so a control can plant against it"
assert control.out contains "descending_free=yes" else "index-row-bound: a shelf whose stamps descend must pass free"
assert control.out contains "descending_counted_zero=yes" else "index-row-bound: a descending shelf must count zero rises rather than merely pass"
assert control.out contains "misordered_bitten=yes" else "index-row-bound: an older row seated above a newer one must red, so no override exists"
assert control.out contains "misordered_counted=yes" else "index-row-bound: the count must name how many seams stand out of order"
assert control.out contains "misordered_named=yes" else "index-row-bound: the seam must be named from both sides, so a hand knows which pair to swap"
assert control.out contains "tie_is_not_misorder=yes" else "index-row-bound: two rows sharing a stamp are the duplicate reading, never this one"
assert control.out contains "tie_stays_duplicate=yes" else "index-row-bound: one fault must be counted once rather than reported by two readings"
assert control.out contains "closed_shelf_order_free=yes" else "index-row-bound: a closed shelf out of order keeps every byte it wrote"
assert control.out contains "closed_shelf_order_uncounted=yes" else "index-row-bound: a closed shelf's order must not be counted against the living gate"
assert control.out contains "bound_applies=yes" else "index-row-bound: the bound read must be the bound applied"
assert control.out contains "control_verdict=ok" else "index-row-bound: the control must agree with itself"
say "forty behaviors proven on planted pins and shelves, each reading shown from both sides"

say "GREEN: index-row-bound -- an index row points; it does not summarise."
